---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import ClaimRecord from "../components/ClaimRecord.astro";
import OutcomeFigure from "../components/OutcomeFigure.astro";
import StatusLedger from "../components/StatusLedger.astro";
import outcomes from "../data/traces/outcomes.json";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { hyphenated, smart } from "../lib/typography.ts";

const title = "Inside AMLO - Niru ML";
const description =
  "Explore the proposed AMLO research procedure, its design illustrations, simulated records, and dated implementation ledger.";
const headline = "Inside AMLO";
const lede =
  "How a study is designed to run, what it records, and what exists today. The procedure is the design AMLO is being built around.";
const notes = (await getCollection("notes")).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.published.valueOf() - a.data.published.valueOf(),
);
const date = (d: Date) =>
  new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-US", { year: "numeric", month: "long", timeZone: "UTC" }).format(d);
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/lab/" currentPath="/lab/">
  <div class="column">
    <h1>
      {
        hyphenated(headline ?? "").map((part) =>
          part.keep ? <span class="nowrap">{part.text}</span> : part.text,
        )
      }
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">{lede}</p>
    <p class="note">
      The diagrams model the design. The claim record is simulated. Neither demonstrates an
      end-to-end AMLO search or a gain at target scale.
    </p>

    <h2 id="problem">Why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      Small-scale evidence can mislead expensive decisions. A design that wins among small models
      can lose at full size. A candidate that trained a little longer, or drew a better learning
      rate, looks like a better architecture. The best of a hundred noisy scores is biased upward,
      because the noise helped to choose it. A careful researcher catches some of these. That care
      is hard to reproduce, hard to audit and does not scale with the number of experiments.
    </p>
    <p>
      A procedure that collects the evidence against a result before it spends more compute gives
      fewer expensive false leads, a clear reason for each allocation of compute, and results that
      a partner can examine instead of take on trust.
    </p>

    <h2 id="first">The first application</h2>
    <p>
      The first kind of study answers a question that comes before every large run. Of these model
      architectures, which one should we train? Candidates are compared at small scale under one
      fixed training recipe and matched compute. A design moves to a larger scale only when the
      evidence at the smaller scale supports it, and it replaces the current best only when it is
      still better at the larger scale.
    </p>

    <h2 id="method">How a study runs</h2>
    <p>
      AMLO is being built around this procedure. Every candidate goes through the same sequence.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Declare.</strong> Before any run, the study states what changes, what stays fixed and
        which resources count.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check.</strong> Each proposal passes a graph check, port checks, scaling checks and a
        static compute estimate. A proposal that fails is rejected before it trains.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Match compute.</strong> Within one comparison, each candidate and the current best get
        the same compute target. A larger model trains for fewer steps, and each run records a receipt
        of the compute it used.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Compare in pairs.</strong> Candidates train from one seed and are scored on the same
        examples. Their losses are compared example by example, so much of the shared noise cancels.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Promote on evidence.</strong> A candidate advances only when the interval for its improvement
        is above the current best, and an exact bound on the bootstrap’s error shows the required confidence
        is met. A Benjamini–Hochberg filter then limits the share of false promotions in each batch.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Promotion has three tiers. Each tier asks one question, with more compute; the later tiers
      ask for stricter confidence than the first.
    </p>
    <dl class="tiers">
      <dt>Tier 1</dt>
      <dd>
        Is it better at the proxy size?
        <span>It trains with the same compute as the current best. 95% confidence.</span>
      </dd>
      <dt>Tier 2</dt>
      <dd>
        Is it reproducibly better?
        <span>A new seed, new examples and five times the compute. 99.9% confidence.</span>
      </dd>
      <dt>Tier 3</dt>
      <dd>
        Is it still better at twice the width?
        <span>
          Both designs train wider, with settings moved by u-μP’s rules, under one compute target.
          99.9% confidence.
        </span>
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Only a candidate that passes Tier 3 replaces the current best. Deterministic checks decide
      what is legal, and a statistical policy decides which candidate gets compute. In a later
      stage, an agent can propose changes, but it cannot decide what passes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="term">What the search edits</h2>
    <p>
      In AMLO’s next design, the whole training step is one typed expression, a term, that holds
      the model, its loss, the gradient derived from them and the update. The search changes a
      model by editing its term, and the cost of each edit is counted exactly from the definitions
      before anything trains. <a href="/#models">The drawing on the home page</a> shows two models written
      this way.
    </p>
    <p>
      An exact representation can make a declared operation count exact. Hardware time and physical
      device work are separate measurements. It does not by itself show that a small model predicts
      a large one. That is a separate question, which Tier 3 asks of every winner and which the <a
        href="/research/spectral-diagnostics/">spectral study</a
      > examines in a toy model.
    </p>

    <h2 id="results">How a result is read</h2>
    <p>
      Each result is an improvement in held-out loss, Δ = reference loss minus candidate loss, with
      an interval around it. Positive means the candidate has lower loss. Where the interval falls
      against the reference gives one of three outcomes.
    </p>
    <OutcomeFigure />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Supported.</strong> The whole interval is above the reference, at the confidence fixed
        before the run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Inconclusive.</strong> The interval crosses the reference. The record keeps the result
        and its reason. It is not a loss.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Refuted.</strong> The whole interval is below the reference.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The final comparison uses a fixed reference, the current best when the search began, so gains
      are never chained from one best to the next. Before it runs, the selection and the analysis
      are frozen. An Auditor, apart from the search, holds a sealed set of examples that the search
      never saw and opens it only after the freeze.
    </p>

    <h2 id="record">What a study records</h2>
    <p>
      A study ends with a claim record, whatever the outcome. The record below comes from a
      simulated run. The site models AMLO’s proposed search rules, and the losses come from a
      synthetic model. The site’s replay checks do not qualify AMLO’s implementation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ClaimRecord />

  <div class="column">
    <p>
      In {outcomes.seeds} seeds of the same simulator, the final comparison is supported in {
        outcomes.supported
      }, inconclusive in {outcomes.inconclusive} and refuted in {outcomes.refuted}. A design that
      wins the search is not certain to win at full size. The record says which happened.
    </p>

    <h2 id="status">Where it stands</h2>
    <p>
      AMLO is a research prototype. Its single-device reference trainer and mathematical foundation
      exist. The search path that enforces matched compute still stops before its first training
      step, and the restricted paths that do train do not yet produce an admitted search result.
      The next milestone is one completed, reproducible search comparison with declared compute
      accounting, followed by independent confirmation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The central claim, that a search run this way picks designs that hold at full size for less
      compute than the usual process, is not yet shown. The tests below are how it will be shown,
      or refuted.
    </p>
    <StatusLedger />

    <h2 id="stages">Where it goes</h2>
    <p>
      Architecture is the first object of the search, not the last. The design widens it in three
      stages, with the same rules for comparison and evidence at each.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Architecture and implementation.</strong> Candidate architectures change in small steps
        under one compute budget.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Training program and curriculum.</strong> The program that trains the model joins the
        search, with its data mixtures and their schedules, filters, objectives, optimizers and the phases
        of training.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deployed model–harness system.</strong> The deployed system joins the search, meaning
        the model with its tools, memory, retrieval, verifiers and the inference resources it can spend.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The long-term aim goes one step further, to a system that applies the same procedure to its
      own method, and keeps a change to how it searches only when a sealed comparison supports it.
      That is a research aim, not a stage of the current plan. It needs its own evidence that each
      change to the method helps.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/research/three-expansions/">Read the program note on the three stages</a></p>

    <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
    <ul class="doc-list">
      {
        notes.map((note) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/research/${note.id}/`}>
              <span class="doc-title">{note.data.title}</span>
              <span class="doc-meta">
                {note.data.status} · {date(note.data.published)}
              </span>
              <span class="doc-summary">{smart(note.data.description)}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2 id="contact">Work with Niru</h2>
    <p>
      Niru ML is an independent research initiative led by Brendon Reperttang, who graduated from
      MIT in Computer Science and Engineering in 2024. <a href="/about/"
        >About Niru and its founder</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p>
      AMLO is private and under development. Researchers, labs, investors and possible partners can
      write to <a href="mailto:brendonrt@niru.ml">brendonrt@niru.ml</a> to discuss the research and see
      the foundation work.
    </p>
    <p><a class="button" href="/preview/">Arrange a conversation</a></p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
